===Alternative forms===
[[he's got his back up]]

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|arcaic}} he is offended or angry; an expression or idea taken from a cat; that animal, when angry, always raising its back. An allusion also sometimes used to jeer a crooked man;
#* {{quote-book|year=1835 |title=The Life and Letters of William Cowper  |author=William Hayley  |page= |ISBN= |passage='''My back is up''', and I cannot hear the thought of wooing him any farther, nor would do it, though he were as pig a gentleman (look you) as Lucifer himself. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2011 |title=The Baby Truce  |author=Jeannie Watt  |page=84 |ISBN=0373717490 |passage=And I want you to be nice to Patty. For some reason '''her back is up'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|year=2014|title=Cabin Girl |author=Kristin Butcher  |page= |ISBN=1459806522 |passage=Now '''my back is up'''. “Yes, the witch,” I hiss. }}
#: ''So, Sir, I see somebody has offended you, for your '''back is up'''.''

